must have heard me loving dying light is  like loving a petulant child it does  good things for a while and you cheer it  on because everything's going so well  and then it gets in trouble it does  something so ridiculous you can only cry  out why are you doing this then again if  you come to dying light because of  developer Techland pedigree this is part  of your expectation a fun game with a  lot of potential a smattering of  frustration and the occasional glitch  but as troubled as dying light off and  ends it's still special and so you stick  with it even when you want to tell it to  go stand in the corner until it's  willing to behave many of the  expectations arise from tech lands Dead  Island games and rightfully so this is a  zombie game with weapon crafting a  stamina meter lots of fetch quests and  plenty of head bludgeoning dying light  however sets itself apart rather quickly  particularly with its parkour system  which sees you running across its two  large urban areas from a first-person  view it takes a short while to get used  to climbing onto ledges which requires  you to be looking at them in the proper  way but then it's off to the races and  you're running across rooftops and  sneering at the zombies below most of  which can't handle a big climb rushing  through the open world this way is fun  and it energizes certain moments of  tension the Far Cry comparisons are  obvious such as with the way you unlock  a few of the game's safe houses by  climbing tall towers but the climbing  requires more finesse than it does in  Far Cry 4 and some of the towers are  outrageously tall making the whole thing  a tense  exercise in precision and tension is the  second aspect of dying light that sets  it apart from its zombie game peers  there are some light-hearted references  and cute visual gags for the most part  this is a dead serious post outbreak  world most important to the sense of  tension is the day/night cycle when  night falls particularly dangerous and  fast zombies roam the city and the  entire timber changes you need to be  wary of the vision cone  of those baddies and avoid direct  confrontation but you'll occasionally  get mobbed in spite of your careful  movement these undead are more  persistent than the Liberty City Police  Department so the best option is to run  run run until you lose them you can hold  a button to look behind you and see how  close they are and talk about tense  moments it's been a while since a zombie  game actually scared me but that look  behind you move reveals some startling  sights throw in a three pronged upgrade  system that makes you stronger and more  agile as the game progresses and you  have the foundation of a great game alas  dying light flounders too often for it  to achieve true greatness though it will  surely develop the same kind of cult  following that Dead Island has earned  the open-air exploration can be  downright terrific particularly in the  second half when you enter the old town  district and encounter the game's best  climbing and leaping paths but when  dying light puts you to work the results  are wildly inconsistent for many hours  of the super long game you're an errand  boy a problem so prevalent that lead  character Kyle crane even remarks upon  it look all I want to do is get this  over with go flip the switch go collect  crayons or mushrooms or coffee or bla  bla bla bla bla bla  the missions get more interesting just  in time to get more frustrating there's  a timed mission in which you plant bombs  while besieged by zombies there's an  ambush by Gunners while you swim in  which it's easy to be killed before you  can even understand what's going on you  get plenty of tools to help you survive  throwing stars tons of melee weapons and  so on but some of these missions are  just set up in such frustrating ways  that you want to shake your fist and  shout what were you thinking to  developer Techland but that's dying  light for you the highs are really high  like player invasions that turn the game  into a competitive arena for a bit you  can adventure cooperatively but it's  this sudden competition in which player  controlled monsters come after you and  try to kill you in freaky ways that adds  real spice to the mix but then the game  will do some weird or bad thing like  getting you stuck between a fence and  the ground or putting you in a tiny area  with a huge ground  pounding thug or obscuring the screen  with so much hazen bloom during a boss  fight that you can only shake your head  but i gotta say the parkour is tight the  world looks good and the story while  cliched got me to actually care about  these people and so i still root for  dying light even when it tries so hard  to dispel my love sorry dying light but  i'm sticking by your side  you
 

